    "body": "## Summary\n\nAdds **ADR-022: Targeted Cascade for Hover** — the specification for the biggest deferred item from ADR-021: re-cascade only the elements whose hover state actually changed, keeping the rest of the `ComputedStyleMap` cached.\n\n**Documentation only.** Implementation (3 commits) lands in a follow-up PR after this spec is reviewed and merged.\n\n## Design grounded in measurement, not estimation\n\nADR-021's Followup section proposed targeted cascade as \"expected <5ms regardless of page size.\" Before writing this ADR, we measured. The instrumentation split `cascade()` into its two passes and reported per-page numbers.\n\n### Measured on Wikipedia HTML (799KB):\n\n| Pass | Time | % of total |\n|---|---|---|\n| `compute_styles` (rule matching + merge) | 664-896ms | **98.5%** |\n| `propagate_inheritance` (top-down inherited props) | 9-13ms | 1.5% |\n| Total per restyle | 673-909ms | 100% |\n\n- Elements: **7,871**\n- Rules: **158** (17 UA + 141 author)\n- Selector match calls: **1,243,618** per cascade (~0.53μs each)\n\n### What this measurement changes\n\nThe ADR-021 Followup was right about the *shape* of the fix (targeted compute), but wrong about the *end-state* number. Once we re-cascade only 2 elements:\n\n- Compute: **~0.2ms** (down from 850ms) — 4,250× reduction\n- **Full propagate stays: ~12ms** (erased entries need inherited properties refilled from parents)\n- **Full restyle_walk stays: ~5ms**\n- **Expected total: ~11-17ms** on Wikipedia\n\n**The honest claim is \"~40× improvement, sub-perceptual threshold\" — NOT the \"<5ms sub-frame\" the ADR-021 Followup aspired to.** This ADR is deliberately undershooting that aspiration because reaching sub-frame requires targeted propagation (which means tracking explicit-vs-inherited per property or re-propagating subtrees — both meaningfully more complex, both deferred).\n\n## What the ADR specifies\n\n### New cascade API (`renderer/css/cascade.hpp`)\n\n```cpp\nvoid cascade_subtree(el, ua_sheet, author_sheet, state, out);\nvoid erase_subtree(el, out);\n// propagate_inheritance exposed publicly (was anon-namespace)\nvoid propagate_inheritance(doc, out);\n```\n\n### RenderPipeline changes\n\n```cpp\nclass RenderPipeline {\n    ComputedStyleMap styles_cache_;\n    Stylesheet sheet_cache_;\n    const dom::Element* prev_hovered_ = nullptr;\n};\n```\n\n- `relayout()` populates the cache (existing full cascade path, unchanged)\n- `render()` clears all three alongside `state_.hovered_element`\n- `restyle()` diffs `prev_hovered_` vs `state_.hovered_element`, does:\n  1. `erase_subtree` for each dirty element\n  2. `cascade_subtree` for each dirty element\n  3. **full** `propagate_inheritance` (refills erased inherited properties)\n  4. **full** `restyle_walk`\n\n## Key design decisions worth reviewing\n\n### 1. `erase_subtree` is necessary (subtle — worth understanding)\n\nWithout it, if `<a>` was already in the cache with inherited `color` from a prior restyle, re-cascading + re-propagating would NOT update its descendants: `propagate_inheritance` only fills properties that are *missing* from the cache entry. Its descendants still have a `color` entry (from before), so propagate skips them.\n\n**Fix:** erase the dirty subtree before re-cascading, so descendants start absent and propagate refills them from the (new) parent.\n\n**Critical correctness invariant:** after targeted restyle, `styles_cache_` must equal what a full cascade would produce with the current state — byte for byte. **This is unit-tested in Commit 2.** If the invariant ever fails, tests fail loudly instead of producing subtle color bugs after shipping.\n\n### 2. Full propagate stays (deferred targeting)\n\nTwo options for targeting propagation exist, both rejected for v1:\n\n- **Track explicit-vs-inherited**: add `bool inherited` to `CascadeValue`. Touches ADR-010's struct.\n- **Re-propagate dirty subtrees**: requires walking each dirty subtree, which for `<a>` is trivial but for `<div>` isn't — and correctness has a nasty edge case (a descendant may inherit from a grandparent outside the dirty subtree).\n\nNeither pays off because propagate is 1.5% of the current cost. Deferred.\n\n### 3. Hover on wrapping `<div>` is potentially slow (documented, not fixed)\n\nIf the hovered element is a large `<div>`, `cascade_subtree(div)` re-cascades the entire subtree. This is correct but potentially expensive. In practice:\n- UA stylesheet only uses `:hover` on `a` (`a:hover { color }`)\n- Wikipedia's inline CSS has no `:hover` rules\n- Hovering a `<div>` produces zero style changes → the subtree re-cascade is wasteful but not incorrect\n\nA future optimization would be \"skip subtree cascade if no `:hover` rule can match the element.\" Deferred.\n\n### 4. Cache invalidation is documented, not just implemented\n\nThe pipeline has a cache, and caches are bugs waiting to happen if the invalidation invariants aren't written down. ADR-022 explicitly documents:\n\n- `render()`: replaces `doc_`, rebuilds cache. Safe.\n- `record_visit()`: **must precede `render()`**. Documented as an invariant that future ADRs must preserve.\n- `relayout()` (width change): rebuilds cache. Safe.\n- `set_hovered_element()`: **does not** invalidate; targeted diff handles it.\n\n## Reviewer checklist\n\n- [ ] **17ms target is the honest number** — not <5ms. Comfortable with the framing? Sub-perceptual (<50ms) is genuinely instant to users; going lower is 17× more work for a delta no human perceives.\n- [ ] **Full propagate stays** — this is why 17ms is the floor. Agree with deferring targeted propagation (which would touch ADR-010's `CascadeValue` struct)?\n- [ ] **Hover on wrapping div is potentially slow** — correct but wasteful; not fixed in v1. Comfortable, or want a `no :hover rule can match` guard now?\n- [ ] **Cache-equality invariant test** — the critical correctness test. Any concern about the invariant being hard to test or flaky?\n- [ ] **Deferred items** — targeted propagation, targeted walk, skip-subtree optimization, Release measurement, cache eviction. All reasonably out of scope?\n\n## Deferred (in ADR, listed here for clarity)\n\n- Targeted propagation (would go from 17ms → <5ms)\n- Targeted walk (5ms → <1ms)\n- Skip subtree cascade when no `:hover` rule can match\n- **Release build measurements** — current numbers are debug (-O0). Release is typically 5-10× faster; if Release gives <5ms, the whole targeted cascade might be unnecessary. But we can't retroactively un-ship it, and it's correct + future-proof.\n- Cache eviction (consistent with ADR-020's unbounded cache approach)\n\n## Test plan\n\nNo code touched, so no test changes. Will run `cmake --build build && ./build/tests/honeybee_tests` after merge to confirm `main` still clean (254 cases, 989 assertions, unchanged).\n\nRefs #145\n",
